   122	## 4. Diagnostics (evaluation only, no search)
   123	
   124	For three rules on seven markets: the base and doubled D2+H3 winners (42 policies) and the base D2 winners (21; the doubled
   125	D2 winners are not included), on the 200 evaluation seeds, re-run with reset and ban instrumentation (63 single-policy
   126	evaluations; the existing gain records carry no reset counts, so none is reused); the selected `x`; per episode the number of resets, the probability of at least one reset, the rounds of resets,
   127	and the rounds spent banned. Check that every D2 winner has zero resets. **Reverse switch (draft 5):** each of the 18 D2 winners re-evaluated with
   128	`x = 1` and every other knob unchanged (18 more evaluations). The diagnostics are built and tested in the driver (`diagnostics`
   129	stage) before any pod. Order: the instrumentation is validated on the registered headline cells before the pod; the
   130	diagnostics of the 18 new D2 winners and their reverse switches run on the Mac after the pod's records are retrieved.
   131	Construction: a D2 winner is embedded in D2+H3 by adding `x` (0 for itself, 1 for the reverse switch); each switch is
   132	the mean difference of normalised profits on the evaluation seeds, the reference cancelling. Resets are the focal
   133	provider's identities of generation 1 or more; ban rounds are the ban lengths imposed on its identities; rounds without
   134	an offer are counted separately. Saved per policy: the seed identifiers, the per-seed normalised profits, the per-seed switch differences, and the
   135	reset and ban counts per episode; a D2 winner with any reset stops the run. Spot check: `fresh0@rho0.2|F111` is also
   136	diagnosed on the pod (`--diag-cells`); the Mac's reset and ban counts must equal the pod's exactly, and its numerical
   137	values must
   138	meet the draft-5 tolerance against it. **Fixed-policy switch:** each of the 42 D2+H3
   139	winners re-evaluated with `x` set to 0 and every other knob unchanged (42 more; a winner that already has `x = 0` is
   140	reported as identical and not re-run); g(winner) - g(winner, x = 0) is the effect of the switch
   141	without re-optimisation, reported beside I.
   142	
   143	## 5. Analysis (fixed now)
   144	
   145	- **Intervals.** Per market: paired seed bootstrap (9,999 resamples, percentile, 95%), the same resampled seed indices for
   146	  every rule and family of that market, so contrasts across rules stay paired. Pooled: equal market weights; in each
   147	  replicate, seeds are resampled independently within each market and the pooled statistic is the mean of the six market
   148	  means; percentile interval. Nominal and descriptive; conditional on the six markets and the frozen policies and
   149	  references.
   150	- **Statistics.** I(r, m) and pooled I(r); the contrasts I(B3) - I(R3) and I(C*) - I(R3); the absolute gaps
   151	  G(D2+H3) = g_B3 - g_R3 under D2+H3 and G(D2) under D2, pooled.
   152	- **Share.** S = 1 - pooled G(D2) / pooled G(D2+H3), a ratio of pooled contrasts, computed in every bootstrap replicate.
   153	  S is reported only if the lower bound of pooled G(D2+H3) exceeds 0.05; otherwise "not defined". No clipping; S can be
   154	  negative or above 1. The absolute gaps are always reported beside it.
   155	- **Market sensitivity (draft 5).** The sign count of I(B3) - I(R3) and I(C*) - I(R3) over the six markets, and S with
   156	  each market left out in turn (six values), each with its own five-market denominator gate (lower bound of the
   157	  five-market pooled G(D2+H3) above 0.05; otherwise that value is "not defined"), for the base and the doubled results
   158	  separately. A nested best-of sensitivity (the better of the D2 and D2+H3 winners per cell) is deferred: it is not
   159	  part of this plan.
   160	- **Doubling flags.** For each D2 cell, the attack change and the reference-reselection change of its doubling check are
   161	  reported separately.
   162	- **Doubled versions** of the same statistics, as in the paper's `tab:ph-doubled`. The base (frozen) searches are primary,
   163	  as in the paper; the doubled versions are reported beside them.
   164	- No confirmatory test, no multiplicity family, no change to any registered decision.
   165	
   166	## 6. Pre-specified expectations and labels
   167	
   168	Written before any fresh D2 result exists. **The six markets, their seeds, and their D2+H3 outcomes were already observed;
   169	only the D2 outcomes are unseen.** This is a descriptive follow-up specified on 2026-11-09 after the round-9 review and
   170	after the headline contrast was seen, not an independent replication.
   171	
   172	Expectations: on all six fresh markets (each market counted; a single market against it fails the expectation), I(B3) > I(R3) and I(C*) > I(R3); pooled S defined and greater than 0.5. Expectations are
   173	scored on point estimates (this is a descriptive follow-up; "met" does not mean that an interval excludes the threshold),
   174	with the denominator gate of section 5 applied separately to the base and the doubled results. Each expectation is
   175	scored as met, failed, or not established: **met** if it holds under both the base and the doubled searches; **failed** if
   176	it fails under both; **not established (search-sensitive)** if the two disagree. An undefined S and a missing cell are
   177	reported as such, separately from failures. A failure is not read as evidence for any particular cause (search error and
   178	mixed outcomes are possible). Only if the pooled-S expectation is met may the paper say: within the D2 family, at the
   179	fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 - R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the
