docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:3:**→ Ambition.** The outside check this paper most needs is an honest-churn arm (honest providers also replace identities), because the main findings depend on its absence; see problem 4.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:19:The paper is honest and its numbers match its outputs. Its conclusions are narrower than its framing. The main result is a null: R3 and a history-based comparator differ by 0.021 in attacker gain. The "equivalent" label comes from a wide margin, and three things can change it: search error as large as the margin, variation between markets, and a comparator that was not selected on gain. The two Q1 "families" are mostly one test. The model has no honest churn, so identity age labels the attacker almost perfectly. The theory is correct but thin. The paper reads as an audit record, not as a finding.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:27:| 1 | Under uniform audits with free replacement, a ban costs nothing | Corollary 1, proof sketch | **Narrower.** True if rivals are honest and do not react, re-entry loses no round, and the rule keeps no identity memory. B2's memory is not checkable. |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:29:| 3 | Priority phase and newest-first targeting are substitutes | Report line 32 | **Supported** for the prescribed searches. The direct reading is stronger: the priority phase adds −0.001 once newest-first targeting is on. |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:39:| Q1 mean gains (C*, R3) | 0.086, 0.065; 0.091, 0.067 | **0.0856, 0.0649; 0.0913, 0.0670, reproduced** | `stability-result.json`, `mean_old` |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:42:| Q2 contrasts | P −0.083, G −0.095, S 0.004, PG 0.082, G given P=0 −0.177, G given P=1 −0.014 | **Reproduced**; conditional effects average to the main effects | Report line 32 |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:46:| Lottery, R3 with H4 | weight 0.4195, gain 0.000374, efficiency 0.932 | **0.00037 and 0.932, reproduced** from the two end points | Report lines 165, 174; Table 14 |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:47:| Lottery, C* with H4 | 0.4845, 0.000456, 0.921 | **0.00044 and 0.921, reproduced** | Same method |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:58:- **Margin.** It is 58 to 77 percent of R3's own gain (0.065 to 0.086). The estimated difference is a 32 percent relative increase (0.0856 / 0.0649).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:81:**4 → With no honest churn, age labels the attacker.**
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:92:- C*'s score is 0.1032 (ledger 3.2). That is an efficiency of 0.993. Gain did not enter the choice (my reasoning).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:100:- Hidden conditions: honest rivals that do not react, re-entry with no lost round, no identity memory in B2's price posting (not checkable).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:118:- P given G = 1 is −0.0014 (report line 32). G alone gives −0.177 of the total −0.178.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:140:- **Dellarocas (2005)**, "Reputation mechanism design in online trading environments with pure moral hazard", Information Systems Research 16(2). doi:10.1287/isre.1050.0054. It treats identity changes. I am fairly sure of the DOI; verify it.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:170:| WEIS 2027 | 2 | 20% | Medium | Add the honest-churn arm and present the paper as a negative result with the newcomer cost |
